================================================================================
MITSUBISHI OEM ACCESSORIES — DATA PIPELINE PLAN
================================================================================


================================================================================
ERROR TAXONOMY
================================================================================

Two error tiers govern the pipeline. Every rule below is tagged with one.

  FATAL
    The sheet cannot be processed. Log the error and skip to the next sheet.
    The pipeline keeps running for remaining sheets.

  DQ_WARNING
    A data quality issue was found. Log the record details to the DQ log and
    continue processing. The offending record may be excluded from output
    depending on the rule (noted per rule below).


================================================================================
CONFIG FILE
================================================================================

All configuration lives in a single file: mitsubishi_config.json
The orchestrator loads this file once at startup and passes each section to the
step that needs it by key (e.g. config["trim_bounds_config"]).

This approach keeps all related values in one place, prevents sections from
drifting out of sync, and makes it straightforward to swap in a different OEM's
config file when building pipelines for other makes.

------------------------------------------------------------
mitsubishi_config.json  —  full structure
------------------------------------------------------------

{
  "column_definition": {
    ...  (see below)
  },
  "required_columns":         [...],
  "non_null_columns":         [...],
  "col_data_type_dict":       {...},
  "trim_bounds_config":       {...},
  "trim_validation_config":   {...},
  "non_acceptable_sheet_names": [...]
}

------------------------------------------------------------
column_definition  (config["column_definition"])
------------------------------------------------------------
Maps incoming column names (which vary across files) to standard internal names.
Matching is always case-insensitive.

{
  "part_number":          { "key_words": { "must_have": ["part", "number"],          "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "english_description":  { "key_words": { "must_have": ["english", "description"],  "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "french_description":   { "key_words": { "must_have": ["french", "description"],   "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "msrp":                 { "key_words": { "must_have": ["msrp"],                    "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "dnp":                  { "key_words": { "must_have": ["dnp"],                     "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "install_time":         { "key_words": { "must_have": ["install", "time"],         "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "labour_rate":          { "key_words": { "must_have": ["labour", "rate"],          "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": [] } },
  "remarks_en":           { "key_words": { "must_have": ["remarks"],                 "must_have_one_of": [], "not_have": ["fr", "french"] } },
  "remarks_fr":           { "key_words": { "must_have": ["remarks"],                 "must_have_one_of": ["fr", "french"], "not_have": [] } }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
required_columns  (config["required_columns"])
------------------------------------------------------------
Columns that must exist after header normalization. If any are missing, FATAL.

["part_number", "english_description", "french_description", "msrp", "dnp", "install_time", "labour_rate"]

------------------------------------------------------------
non_null_columns  (config["non_null_columns"])
------------------------------------------------------------
After header promotion, these columns must have no null values.

["part_number", "english_description", "french_description", "msrp", "dnp", "install_time", "labour_rate"]

------------------------------------------------------------
col_data_type_dict  (config["col_data_type_dict"])
------------------------------------------------------------
Used in Step 3 to enforce data types on standard columns.

{
  "to_string": ["part_number", "english_description", "french_description"],
  "to_float":  ["msrp", "dnp", "install_time", "labour_rate"]
}

------------------------------------------------------------
trim_bounds_config  (config["trim_bounds_config"])
------------------------------------------------------------
Defines the boundary columns that bracket the trim-level columns.

{
  "left_bound":  { "must_contain": ["remarks"], "must_contain_one_of": [] },
  "right_bound": { "must_contain": [], "must_contain_one_of": ["photo", "image"] }
}

------------------------------------------------------------
trim_validation_config  (config["trim_validation_config"])
------------------------------------------------------------
Used to confirm that candidate columns are truly trim-level columns by their data.

{
  "expected_data":  { "data": "X", "perc_thresh": 60 },
  "must_have_only": { "expected_value_types": ["X", "", "__na__"] }
}

  Notes on this config:
  - All string comparisons are case-insensitive (so "x" matches "X").
  - "__na__" is a sentinel value. When the validator encounters it, it resolves
    the check to pd.isna(value) == True, covering both None and float NaN.
  - perc_thresh: the percentage of values in a candidate column that must equal
    the expected_data value for the column to pass as a valid trim column.

------------------------------------------------------------
non_acceptable_sheet_names  (config["non_acceptable_sheet_names"])
------------------------------------------------------------
List of sheet names that are always skipped regardless of name format.
Example: ["Summary", "Instructions", "Cover Page"]


================================================================================
SHARED HELPERS
================================================================================

These functions are used by multiple steps. Define them once in a shared module.

------------------------------------------------------------
promote_header_row(df) -> df
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Creates a copy of df to avoid mutating the original.
  - Drops the first row (index 0).
  - Resets the index.
  - Promotes the new first row (which was the original second row) to be the
    column headers.
  - Returns the df with promoted headers.

  Used by: Step 1 (data completeness), Step 3 (standardization).
  This is the single definition — do not re-implement this logic elsewhere.

------------------------------------------------------------
lowercase_columns(col_list: list) -> list
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Takes a list of column name strings.
  - Returns each name lowercased, preserving the original order.

  Used by: Step 2 (header normalization).


================================================================================
DQ LOG SCHEMA
================================================================================

Every DQ_WARNING must be written as a record to the DQ log with these fields:

  run_id            : Unique identifier for this pipeline run (e.g. UUID or timestamp)
  source_file       : Name of the Excel file being processed
  sheet_name        : Name of the worksheet
  model_name        : Value from cell (row=0, col=0)
  record_index      : Row index of the offending record in the working df
  record_snapshot   : Full dict of the record's field values at the time of the check
  rule_violated     : Machine-readable rule name (e.g. "profitability_rule", "trim_applicability_rule")
  issue_description : Human-readable explanation of what was wrong


================================================================================
STEP 1 — DATA EVALUATION (VALIDATION)
================================================================================

Input  : Raw Excel worksheet loaded as a DataFrame (original row structure intact)
Output : meta_data dict populated with sheet-level information, and working_df
         (header-promoted copy of the sheet) ready for Step 2.
         OR raises FATAL and skips the sheet.
Side effects: DQ_WARNINGs written to the DQ log for profitability violations.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.1  Sheet Name Validation
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: FATAL — skip this sheet if it fails, continue to next sheet.

  - Check that the worksheet name matches the pattern: YYYY <string>
    (four digits, one space, followed by one or more non-space characters).
  - Check that the worksheet name is NOT in config["non_acceptable_sheet_names"].
  - If either check fails, log a FATAL with the sheet name and reason, then skip.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.2  Structural Validation
------------------------------------------------------------

  1.2.1  Model name check
  Error type: FATAL

    - Assert that cell (row=0, col=0) is non-null and contains a string value.
    - Store the value in meta_data["model_name"].

  1.2.2  Header keywords check
  Error type: FATAL

    - Examine row index 1 of the raw df (the second row).
    - Confirm (case-insensitive) that ALL of the following keywords appear
      somewhere in that row:
        ['Part Number', 'English Description', 'French Description',
         'Remarks', 'MSRP', 'DNP', 'Install Time', 'Labour Rate']
    - If any keyword is missing, log FATAL listing which keywords were not found.

  1.2.3  Trim boundary marker check
  Error type: FATAL

    - In row index 1 (the header row), confirm there is a column whose name
      contains "Remarks" (case-insensitive). This is the LEFT boundary marker.
    - Confirm there is a column whose name contains "Photo" or "Image"
      (case-insensitive). This is the RIGHT boundary marker.
    - Do NOT extract trim columns here. This check only confirms the boundaries
      exist so Step 2 can do the full extraction.
    - If either boundary is missing, log FATAL.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.3  Data Completeness Validation
------------------------------------------------------------

  Call promote_header_row(df) to get working_df.
  All checks below operate on working_df.

  Note: Column names in working_df are still raw/original at this stage.
  Use case-insensitive partial matching against raw column names when
  locating the columns listed below.

  1.3.1  Non-null columns check
  Error type: FATAL

    - For each column in config["non_null_columns"], assert there are no null
      values in working_df.
    - If any column has nulls, log FATAL with the column name and null count.

  1.3.2  Column data type check
  Error type: FATAL

    - String columns: ['Part Number', 'English Description', 'French Description']
      Assert all non-null values in these columns are strings.
    - Numeric columns: ['MSRP', 'DNP', 'Install Time', 'Labour Rate']
      Assert all non-null values in these columns are coercible to float.
    - If any check fails, log FATAL with the column name and a sample of
      the offending values.

------------------------------------------------------------
1.4  Profitability Rule Check
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: DQ_WARNING — log offending records, continue processing.

  - Coerce the DNP and MSRP columns in working_df to float before comparing.
  - For each record:
    - If DNP <= 0 or MSRP <= 0, write a DQ_WARNING.
    - If MSRP <= DNP, write a DQ_WARNING.
  - Include in the DQ_WARNING: rule_violated = "profitability_rule",
    plus the full record_snapshot.
  - These records are NOT excluded from the pipeline at this stage.
    They are flagged for review but still processed.


================================================================================
STEP 2 — HEADER NORMALIZATION
================================================================================

Input  : working_df (header-promoted, from Step 1),
         config["column_definition"],
         config["trim_bounds_config"],
         config["trim_validation_config"],
         config["required_columns"]

Output :
  {
    "col_mapping":          { "original_col_name": "mapped_col_name | None", ... },
    "valid_trim_cols":      ["trim_col_A", "trim_col_B", ...],
    "trim_validation_log":  { ... }
  }

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: column_type_finder(col_name, column_definition) -> dict
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Lowercase col_name before any matching.
  - Iterate through each key in column_definition.
  - For each key, apply the three rules in this sequence:
      1. not_have   : if ANY keyword in this list appears in col_name → skip key
      2. must_have  : ALL keywords in this list must appear in col_name
      3. must_have_one_of : if the list is non-empty, AT LEAST ONE keyword must
                            appear in col_name
  - Return {"<original_col_name>": "<matched_key>"} for the first key that
    passes all three rules.
  - If no key matches, return {"<original_col_name>": None}.

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: map_all_columns(df, column_definition) -> dict
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Call lowercase_columns(df.columns) to get lowercased names for matching,
    while keeping the original column names as keys in the output dict.
  - Call column_type_finder for each column.
  - Return a single dict of all results:
    { "Part Number": "part_number", "MSRP": "msrp", "Unnamed: 5": None, ... }

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: assert_required_columns(col_mapping, required_cols_list) -> None or FATAL
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: FATAL

  - Collect all non-None values from col_mapping into a flat list.
  - Check that every entry in required_cols_list (config) is present in that list.
  - If any required column is missing:
    - Log FATAL. Include which required columns had no match and which original
      column names mapped to None (these are the unrecognized columns).
    - Raise the error to halt processing of this sheet.

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: identify_trim_candidates(df, trim_bounds_config) -> list
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: FATAL if bounds cannot be located.

  - Lowercase df.columns for matching.
  - Find the index of the LEFT bound column using must_contain and
    must_contain_one_of rules from trim_bounds_config["left_bound"].
  - Find the index of the RIGHT bound column using the same rules from
    trim_bounds_config["right_bound"].
  - If either bound cannot be located, log FATAL.
  - Return the list of column names whose index is strictly between the
    left and right bound indices (the bounds themselves are excluded).

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: validate_trim_by_datatype(df, candidates, trim_validation_config) -> (list, dict)
------------------------------------------------------------

  For each column in candidates:

    a) must_have_only check:
       - Get the list of expected_value_types from the config.
       - For each expected type, resolve "__na__" to a pd.isna() check.
         All other values are compared case-insensitively as strings.
       - Assert that every value in the candidate column matches one of
         the expected value types (after resolving __na__).

    b) expected_data rate check:
       - Count what percentage of values in the column equal the
         expected_data["data"] value (case-insensitive comparison).
       - The column passes if that percentage >= perc_thresh.

    Record the result for each candidate column:
    {
      "<col_name>": {
        "result":             "pass" | "fail",
        "expected_data_rate": <float, e.g. 0.72>,
        "unique_col_data":    [<list of unique values found in the column>]
      }
    }

  - Columns that fail are excluded from the pipeline.
  - Log each failed candidate as a DQ_WARNING with rule_violated =
    "trim_candidate_failed_validation".
  - Return (passed_cols_list, full_results_dict).

------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 Main Function
------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Call map_all_columns(working_df, config["column_definition"])           -> col_mapping
  2. Call assert_required_columns(col_mapping, config["required_columns"])  [FATAL if fails]
  3. Call identify_trim_candidates(working_df, config["trim_bounds_config"]) -> candidates
  4. Call validate_trim_by_datatype(working_df, candidates, config["trim_validation_config"])
       -> (valid_trim_cols, trim_validation_log)
  5. Return:
     {
       "col_mapping":         col_mapping,
       "valid_trim_cols":     valid_trim_cols,
       "trim_validation_log": trim_validation_log
     }


================================================================================
STEP 3 — DATA STANDARDIZATION
================================================================================

Input  : working_df (from Step 1),
         Step 2 output dict (col_mapping, valid_trim_cols),
         config["col_data_type_dict"]
Output : standardized_df

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: apply_col_mapping(df, col_mapping) -> df
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Rename columns in df using the col_mapping dict (original -> mapped name).
  - Drop any columns whose mapped value is None (unrecognized columns),
    EXCEPT for columns that are in valid_trim_cols — those are kept as-is
    since they were identified by position, not by name matching.
  - Return the renamed df.

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: enforce_col_data_types(df, col_data_type_dict) -> df
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: FATAL if conversion fails.

  - col_data_type_dict is config["col_data_type_dict"] and has the structure:
      { "to_string": [...col names...], "to_float": [...col names...] }
  - For each column in to_string: convert non-null values to str.
  - For each column in to_float: convert non-null values to float.
  - Skip null / NaN values during conversion.
  - If any conversion raises an error, log FATAL with the column name and
    a sample of values that could not be converted.
  - Return the type-enforced df.

------------------------------------------------------------
Helper: standardize_trim_values(df, trim_cols) -> df
------------------------------------------------------------
  - For each column in trim_cols:
    - Convert all non-null string values to uppercase.
    - Leave null / empty values unchanged.
  - Return the df.

------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 Main Function
------------------------------------------------------------
  1. Call promote_header_row(working_df)                -> promoted_df
  2. Call apply_col_mapping(promoted_df, col_mapping)   -> mapped_df
  3. Call enforce_col_data_types(mapped_df, config["col_data_type_dict"])  -> typed_df  [FATAL if fails]
  4. Call standardize_trim_values(typed_df, valid_trim_cols)     -> standardized_df
  5. Return standardized_df


================================================================================
STEP 4 — DATA TRANSFORMATION (PIVOT TRIM LEVELS)
================================================================================

Input  : standardized_df, valid_trim_cols list
Output : transformed_df — one row per valid part-trim combination

------------------------------------------------------------
4.1  Part Applicability Validation
------------------------------------------------------------
Error type: DQ_WARNING — offending records are excluded from output.

  - For each record in standardized_df, check whether at least one column in
    valid_trim_cols contains "X" (case-insensitive).
  - If a record has no "X" across all trim columns, it applies to no trim level.
  - Write a DQ_WARNING for that record with:
    rule_violated = "trim_applicability_rule"
    issue_description = "Record applies to zero trim levels"
  - Exclude these records from the df before proceeding to the melt.

------------------------------------------------------------
4.2  Melt Trim Columns
------------------------------------------------------------
  - Use pd.melt to unpivot the trim level columns:
      id_vars     = all non-trim columns in standardized_df
      value_vars  = valid_trim_cols
      var_name    = "trim_level"
      value_name  = "applicable"
  - Filter the melted df to keep only rows where applicable == "X"
    (case-insensitive).
  - Drop the "applicable" column after filtering.
  - Return transformed_df.


================================================================================
STEP 5 — OUTPUT
================================================================================

Input  : transformed_df, meta_data dict
Output : Data written to the target destination defined in config.

  5.1  Enrich with run metadata
       - Add the following columns to transformed_df from meta_data:
           model_name, sheet_name, source_file
       - Add run_id for traceability.

  5.2  Write output
       - Write transformed_df to the target (DB table or CSV — path/table
         defined in config["output"]).


================================================================================
STEP 6 — DQ REPORT SUMMARY
================================================================================

Run once after all sheets have been processed.

  - Aggregate all DQ_WARNINGs collected during the run.
  - Produce a per-sheet summary:
      sheet_name, total_records_in, records_excluded, warning_count_by_rule
  - Write the full DQ log and the summary to the path defined in config["output"].


================================================================================
FULL PIPELINE EXECUTION ORDER
================================================================================

For each Excel file:
  For each worksheet in the file:

    1.  [STEP 1.1]  Sheet name validation          FATAL -> skip sheet
    2.  [STEP 1.2]  Structural validation           FATAL -> skip sheet
    3.  [STEP 1.3]  Data completeness validation    FATAL -> skip sheet
    4.  [STEP 1.4]  Profitability rule check        DQ_WARNING -> log, continue
    5.  [STEP 2  ]  Header normalization            FATAL -> skip sheet
    6.  [STEP 3  ]  Data standardization            FATAL -> skip sheet
    7.  [STEP 4  ]  Transform / pivot trim levels   DQ_WARNING for bad records
    8.  [STEP 5  ]  Write output

  End for each worksheet.

  [STEP 6]  Write DQ report summary for this file.

End for each file.
